    "result_text": "Tests still green. Here's how I addressed each finding.\n\n## Finding 2 (duplicated household traversal) \u2014 fixed\n\nExtracted a single `householdMembers(owner)` helper that owns the `findAllOwners().stream().filter(... householdId ...)` traversal. Both `stampHouseholdSize` and `capMembershipLevelToHousehold` now consume it, so \"who is in this household\" is defined in one place.\n\nI deliberately did **not** fold the deleted-filter into the shared helper, as the review suggested wording it (\"returns the non-deleted same-household owners\"). Doing so would silently change `stampHouseholdSize`: it currently counts *all* same-household owners (including soft-deleted) into `householdSize`, which feeds `membershipPoints`. The helper therefore returns all members and each caller applies its own policy \u2014 `stampHouseholdSize` keeps counting everyone (behavior unchanged), the cap filters to live members (a deleted/retired owner shouldn't raise a new member's ceiling). This centralizes the real duplication the finding targets while keeping both behaviors exactly as before.\n\n## Finding 1 (removal of `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate`) \u2014 left removed, with reasoning\n\nI disagree that this guard can be restored while keeping the acceptance test green, so I've left it removed and want to be explicit about why.\n\nThe guard throws `DuplicateHouseholdException` whenever an **undeclared** owner joins a household that already has a member. The new test creates exactly that: two owners with the same `lastName`+`postcode` (same `householdId`), second one without `sharesHousehold`, and asserts **both** creates return 2xx. With the guard in place, the second create is a 409 and the test fails (I verified this \u2014 it's the failure I originally hit).\n\nThe cap feature's precondition *is* coexisting household members at create time. The guard's rejection forbids precisely that scenario, so the two are in direct conflict rather than orthogonal \u2014 this isn't unrelated collateral. And there's no surviving sub-case: every household join now resolves to \"admit and cap,\" so a narrowed guard would never fire, leaving dead code that still re-breaks the test. True re-registrations (identical telephone/email/household) remain rejected by `requireUniqueIdentity`, which its own Javadoc already calls \"the single consolidated duplicate check.\"\n\nWhat I *did* keep conservative: the `DuplicateHouseholdException` class and its `ExceptionControllerAdvice` mapping are untouched \u2014 I only removed the dead call, method, and import from the controller, so nothing unrelated was deleted wholesale.\n\nIf the intent is genuinely to preserve the hard household-duplicate rejection, that's incompatible with this acceptance test as written and would need the test to opt in via `sharesHousehold` \u2014 a change to the spec/test rather than the implementation. Happy to go that route if you'd prefer it.",
